  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "eYx3KppB1tYs9O3CGfKdz8dIjcw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:32.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## A package can ship its own offline story\n\nA package declares PWA defaults in the `powerhouse.manifest.json` it publishes, using the same shape as `connect.pwa`, so installing it works offline out of the box:\n\n```json\n{ \"name\": \"@acme/todos\",\n  \"pwa\": { \"runtimeCaching\": [\n    { \"urlPattern\": { \"source\": \"^https://api\\\\.acme\\\\.io/\" }, \"handler\": \"NetworkFirst\" } ] } }\n```\n\nThe build collects fragments from the project's own root manifest, from bundled packages (`provider: \"local\"`, read from `node_modules`), and from non-bundled packages (`registry`, `npm`, `github`, or omitted) fetched from the registry CDN using the same version-pinned spec Connect loads at runtime. An unreachable registry is not fatal: the build warns, skips the fragment, and succeeds. A malformed fragment is likewise warned about (naming package and field) and skipped.\n\n## The merge rule is the design decision\n\nPrecedence runs `Connect defaults < package fragments (declared order) < the project's own manifest < project connect.pwa`, with per-field-type strategies:\n\n| Field type | Strategy |\n| --- | --- |\n| Manifest scalars, incl. `launch_handler` | Deep-merge \u2014 **project wins** |\n| `icons`, `file_handlers`, derived `categories`, globs, `runtimeCaching`, denylist | **Additive** |\n| `maximumFileSizeToCacheInBytes` | The **max** across layers |\n\nAdditive arrays are the point: a package can always *add* offline coverage, and no later layer can quietly remove it. When two packages set the same scalar differently and the project does not settle it, the build warns and last-loaded wins \u2014 pin it in `connect.pwa` to decide explicitly.\n\n`file_handlers` hands the OS a launch, so it is constrained: a fragment declares only *what* it accepts, never *where* it opens (a fragment setting its own `action` fails validation), extensions must start with a dot, and contributed entries are appended after Connect's built-in `.phd`/`.phdm` handler \u2014 browsers give each extension to the first claimant, so a package cannot hijack the built-in association.\n\nPackages installed **at runtime** are not baked into the worker but are not ignored either: the worker serves the web-app manifest dynamically and merges their fragments in. Two extra guardrails apply there, since the end user rather than the deployer chose them \u2014 `start_url` and `scope` stay Connect's, and built-in handlers stay first.\n\n_Source: `apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/05-Launch/06-ConfigurePWA.md`, Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:32.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:32.000Z"
    }
  },
